{"name":"Bitter Orange","hex":"#d5762b","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bitter orange","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A citrus tree of the hybrid species Citrus × aurantium, also called Citrus aurantium.","A citrus tree of the species Citrus limetta."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bitter_orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bitter orange","blocks":[{"text":"The bitter orange, sour orange, Seville orange, bigarade orange, or marmalade orange is the hybrid citrus tree species Citrus × aurantium, and its fruit. It is native to Southeast Asia and has been spread by humans to many parts of the world. It is a cross between the pomelo, Citrus maxima, and the wild type mandarin orange, Citrus reticulata. The bitter orange is used to make essential oil, used in foods, drinks, and pharmaceuticals. The Seville orange is prized for making British orange marmalade."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Citrus_aurantium.jpg/330px-Citrus_aurantium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitter_orange","wikidata":"Q147096"},{"title":"Orange (fruit)","blocks":[{"text":"The orange, also called sweet orange to distinguish it from the bitter orange, is the fruit of a tree in the family Rutaceae. Botanically, this is the hybrid Citrus × sinensis, between the pomelo and the mandarin orange. The chloroplast genome, and therefore the maternal line, is that of pomelo. Hybrids of the sweet orange form later types of mandarin and the grapefruit. The sweet orange has had its full genome sequenced."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Oranges_-_whole-halved-segment.jpg/330px-Oranges_-_whole-halved-segment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(fruit)","wikidata":"Q13191"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Citrus_aurantium_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-042.jpg/330px-Citrus_aurantium_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-042.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Citrus_aurantium_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-042.jpg/330px-Citrus_aurantium_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-042.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Foliage, blossoms and fruit. Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, 1897","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACitrus_aurantium_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-042.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg/250px-Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg/330px-Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg/250px-Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm